「The Throne」— Nucleus of the Root (根源の核・玉座, Kongen no Kaku Gyokuza)—is the designation given to the incomprehensible intrinsic nucleus of 「　」 within the Nasuverse cosmology, the relationship through which differentiated creation remains governable, distinguishable, and ultimately capable of returning to its absolute source. It has received numerous interpretations, including the Divine Throne, the Throne of God, the Divine Right of Kings, the Throne of Glory, the Mercy Seat, and the Footstool. These names express the attempts of observers to represent something whose nature cannot be exhaustively contained within the concepts available to them. Even the designation “Throne” is a useful mistranslation, presenting an incomprehensible relationship through the familiar imagery of a seat from which many subordinate laws receive direction. Its true nature need not resemble furniture, architecture, a chamber, or an identifiable object. One observer might perceive a throne, another a singularity, another a divine court, and another an equation whose solution cannot be expressed. Such representations reveal something of the observer’s means of understanding without establishing that any particular appearance is the complete form of the nucleus itself. The Throne belongs to Akasha so fundamentally that treating them as separable existences already misrepresents their relationship. It possesses no independent standing above the Root, and its description as a nucleus does not establish a detachable component contained within a greater object. Terms such as deepest, within, summit, and vacancy supply approximations for a relationship to which ordinary spatial distinctions cannot adequately apply. The Throne is conventionally described as existing beyond the World and every dimensional framework, yet even the distinction between inside and outside belongs to the differentiated creation whose boundaries it maintains. Its incomprehensibility therefore concerns more than an observer’s inability to discover enough information. Language, mathematics, causal reasoning, dimensional theory, and the distinction between observer and observed are themselves expressions of a reality already sufficiently differentiated for those concepts to function. Using them to define the Throne exhaustively would mean attempting to contain the condition permitting distinctions through distinctions dependent upon that condition. Its nature cannot be reduced to an especially complex structure, a sufficiently distant higher dimension, or an ordinary first cause placed at the beginning of a causal sequence. Causality, sequence, beginning, and structure already presuppose the applicability of distinctions. The Throne maintains the primary relationship through which something emerging from the intrinsic Nothingness of 「　」 can remain a differentiated phenomenon. A soul can remain a particular soul, a possibility can remain distinguishable from another possibility, and a world can possess its own history without immediately becoming indistinguishable from everything else sharing the same ultimate source. The unity of origin does not prevent the plurality of what emerges from it because differentiation is maintained. Existence and nonexistence, inside and outside, unity and plurality, infinite and finite, life and death, individuality and totality, soul and body, past and future, possibility and impossibility, cause and effect, and Origin and bearer can consequently become mutually meaningful distinctions throughout creation. Order and disorder alike depend upon this relationship: the Throne’s function extends to the conditions through which either can possess a distinguishable character. It does not require a moral judgment that order is good or disorder is evil. Akasha’s status as the source and destination of phenomena does not establish an ethical preference among those phenomena, and the Throne’s maintenance of differentiation should not be mistaken for such a preference. Its role permits a thing to possess properties and remain sufficiently determinate for those properties to mean anything. Without this relationship, the distinctions through which phenomena possess recognizable form, causal history, orientation, or identity would lack the stability necessary to remain applicable. The familiar claim that the Throne is immune to every concept is therefore inadequate. Immunity already presupposes an applicable category and an existence that resists its operation. The Throne cannot be exhaustively placed within the categories whose applicability it maintains, nor can this difficulty be resolved by assigning it a mysterious third position beyond a simple opposition. Its relationship with distinctions is intrinsic to the conditions through which the opposing positions become meaningful in differentiated creation at all. Even declaring it indescribable remains a representation produced through the same limited means. The possibility of perceiving a meaningful image of it is consequently remarkable in itself, because the image permits an observer to approach something that the image cannot faithfully contain. As the nucleus of the past, present, and future, infinite parallel universes, higher-dimensional planes, and all laws, the Throne maintains the relationship through which those domains remain distinct while ultimately sharing the same source. One universe can possess a causal history different from another, matter can remain distinguishable from information, and an individual can remain distinguishable from the World. These distinctions continue to matter while the relevant phenomena remain differentiated; upon complete return to 「　」, their separate applicability ceases. The Throne governs the integrity of that return, preserving the relationship through which souls can return as wholes instead of becoming dispersed fragments whose proper return would be lost. It likewise stabilizes Origins emerging from the Root. An Origin constitutes the fundamental orientation of an existence, while its eventual bearer is the particular individual whose life proceeds under that orientation. Their distinction must remain sufficiently stable for the person to possess an individual existence rather than being immediately overwhelmed by the fundamental direction preceding their birth. Stabilized Origins are consequently maintained in dormancy before their bearers are born. Awakening an Origin weakens the distinction between orientation and individual without necessarily abolishing it. The Throne’s relationship with Origins encompasses their emergence, stabilization, and eventual return, while the bearer remains a particular person rather than merely an interchangeable expression of an abstract direction. These functions also explain why the Throne’s standing cannot be adequately measured through the miracles available within creation. True Magic can realize what would otherwise be impossible under the World’s conditions, but the distinction between possibility and impossibility remains among the distinctions maintained through the nucleus. This relationship does not mean that the Throne personally operates every True Magic or that its ruler automatically acquires each Magician’s abilities. It concerns the applicability of the conditions through which a miracle can be distinguished from the ordinary operation of the World. The function most directly associated with divine sovereignty is its capacity to serve as the seat of a ruling Throne God. Such a God possesses an Origin that has attained Taikyoku, becoming a Supreme Ultimate Origin capable of emanating outward and establishing its own reality throughout creation. The Origin supplies the fundamental principle, while the God’s Craving determines the arrangement through which that principle becomes a Hegemony Law. Through the Throne, the individual’s inner universe receives the support necessary to establish that Law as the governing order of the painted creation. The previous physics, temporal relationships, concepts of life and death, and other conditions of existence can be superseded by the ruler’s personal truth. This establishment extends beyond a local alteration of the environment, reaching the terms under which the World and the domains encompassed by the God’s dominion are permitted to operate. The imagery of painting conveys the relationship between the supreme principle, the God expressing it, and the creation receiving it. The Throne supplies the intrinsic mechanism of establishment and stabilization; the particular world expressed through it proceeds from the ruler’s own Origin and desire. Its underlying functions must therefore remain distinguishable from the personal Law occupying it. Mercurius’s recurrence belongs to Acta Est Fabula and the creation called Omega Eternality, while the Throne’s maintenance of differentiation, stabilization, and succession cannot be reduced to recurrence alone. The seat also preserves the relationship between its ruler and the Absolute Origin. When an individual Origin becomes Taikyoku, the distinction between that Supreme Ultimate Origin and 「　」 becomes infinitesimally small. The Throne stabilizes the remaining distinction, permitting the God to retain an independent consciousness instead of dissolving completely into undifferentiated Akasha. The ruler’s proximity is expressed through the language of a final restriction before absolute oneness, although neither proximity nor restriction should be understood as a measurable physical interval. The continued distinction is what allows the God to remain someone who desires, perceives, rejects, and governs. Occupancy consequently does not make the ruler the Root itself, and complete disappearance of that distinction would erase the separate individuality through which their Law possesses a personal direction. The Throne simultaneously supports the overwhelming pressure of the God’s existence. A fully established Throne God possesses a metaphysical weight that the All of Creation cannot withstand; unrestricted manifestation would tear apart the space, time, and matter receiving it. The seat contains and stabilizes this pressure while allowing emanation to establish an ordered domain. All of Creation is thereby protected from the very existence governing it. This support should not be interpreted as the action of an ordinary object strong enough to bear a heavy occupant. The seat represents the relationship through which the God’s supreme existence and the continued differentiation of the painted world remain stable together. Its stabilizing function also preserves the established creation after the death of its ruler. Although the world expresses the emanation of a particular God, the disappearance of that God does not automatically erase everything already painted. The existing order remains established through the Throne until another qualified God successfully claims the seat and imposes a new Hegemony Law. Killing a ruler, destroying their established world, and completing a permanent succession are therefore separate outcomes. The possibility of succession belongs to the structure of divine rulership, although no challenger has permanently displaced the current ruler. Access to the Throne introduces an ordeal distinct from ordinary travel or the acquisition of great magical power. Its nature provides no destination within the World that can be approached through walking, flight, teleportation, or movement between dimensions. Such methods presuppose meaningful coordinates, distance, and a destination belonging to the framework being traversed. A genuine approach requires a Singular Finite Curve, a rupture opened by tearing through the canvas of reality and entering a vacancy where the conditions of differentiated creation cease to provide an ordinary route. Its description as a space of nothingness conveys the point at which reality has exhausted the framework through which further movement would normally be understood. The apparent depth of the Curve expresses how few of creation’s conditions remain applicable between the challenger and the nucleus, together with the metaphysical difficulty of maintaining the approach. Ordinarily, opening such a route involves establishing a path to the Root, already an undertaking of extraordinary difficulty within magecraft. Reaching Akasha does not independently guarantee access to its intrinsic nucleus, and the probability of such a path reaching the Throne remains exceedingly small. The necessary extent of the rupture is influenced by the strength of the ruling God, with a stronger ruler demanding a more profound breach before the nucleus can be approached. A sufficiently intense clash between incompatible Hegemony Laws can also tear open a Singular Finite Curve, as the competing supreme principles become unable to establish mutually exclusive realities without rupturing the creation receiving them. Producing the opening does not guarantee survival of its traversal. The challenger must endure the approach before encountering the Throne’s autonomous defensive mechanism, the Judgement of Mortality. This manifests as an annihilating blast of the Throne’s own killing intent, directed against those attempting to claim its seat and capable of erasing a struck existence without leaving a trace. Its significance extends beyond an attack intended to inflict bodily injury. The ordeal can be understood as an intrinsic consequence of attempting to introduce a differentiated self and Origin into the inseparable nucleus of 「　」. The language of defense and judgment describes how the event appears to the challenger without requiring an ordinary mind evaluating moral worth. Survival of the approach, overcoming the Judgement, confrontation with the ruler, and qualification to establish a new Law remain separate requirements. A path to the Root is therefore no universal entitlement to divine sovereignty. Mercurius first reached the Throne as a mage, barely surviving the Judgement before claiming the seat and establishing the creation governed by his recurrence. His successful occupancy demonstrates that an individual can attain this relationship without converting its intrinsic functions into a creation of that individual. The distinction likewise matters when considering 「Shiki Ryougi」. Her direct connection with Akasha establishes a relationship to the same absolute source, but connection does not automatically establish occupancy, possession of the Throne’s governing functions, or passage through its ordeal of succession. The manner in which she relates to 「　」 need not coincide with the conceptual framework through which a Throne God experiences rulership. Conversely, the ruler’s occupancy grants no ownership over the Root merely because the seat stabilizes Origins and establishes a personal Law. The nucleus also preserves records conventionally called the Memories of the Throne. These concern the configurations of existence that have passed through its governing relationship: iterations, World-Transformations, established Laws, rulers, templates of reality, and individuals particularly connected with the seat and its succession. Their relationship with the wider Akashic Records is intimate without making the two designations completely synonymous. The Akashic Records concern reality at the level of Akasha, while the Memories specifically express what has passed through the nucleus and the orders it preserves. The existence of these records does not establish unrestricted access for every occupant; Mercurius’s paradoxical condition prevents his use of them despite his rulership. The many religious names associated with the Throne can similarly be understood as distorted impressions of an incomprehensible relationship translated into familiar symbolism. A throne, court, sovereign right, or place of mercy may preserve an observer’s encounter with something real while remaining an incomplete representation of what was encountered. Even Unified Language may be unable to describe its nature faithfully, because a language belonging to the World remains dependent upon the distinctions whose applicability the nucleus maintains. Every effort to approach its truth must therefore pass through representations already inadequate to contain it. The closer an observer comes to the intrinsic relationship denoted by 「The Throne」, the less completely the words throne, nucleus, mechanism, and even existence can account for what those words have permitted them to glimpse.
